1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,143
My name is James Rosing.

2
00:00:05,143 --> 00:00:11,143
I'm a physician and surgeon in Newport
Beach, California, and this is my entry.

3
00:00:11,143 --> 00:00:19,286
Everything you'll see, I built this
week, on top of my own record system,
appeal engine, and coverage index.

4
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,014
The problem is simple.

5
00:00:21,014 --> 00:00:27,014
When an insurer denies a claim, a
machine does it in about a second.

6
00:00:27,014 --> 00:00:32,157
If the physician appeals, they do it by
hand, which takes hours.

7
00:00:32,157 --> 00:00:35,586
For this reason most denials are never
answered.

8
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:49,743
This is one of fifty synthetic cases
without any real patient data: a
47-year-old woman undergoes radiation
planning after lumpectomy for breast
cancer, denied by her Medicare Advantage
plan as not medically necessary.

9
00:00:49,743 --> 00:00:57,886
Her chart is on the left, the insurance
plan's rules in the middle, and the
appeal on the right.

10
00:00:57,900 --> 00:00:58,757
Now watch:

11
00:00:58,757 --> 00:01:06,900
Spark 1.3 asks my coverage index when
this plan's rules last changed, then
opens only the entries it needs.

12
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:11,186
Every sentence it writes cites one entry
and quotes it.

13
00:01:11,186 --> 00:01:20,186
Then a deterministic gate verifies the
quote is in that entry and every number,
date, code, and side is sourced
accurately.

14
00:01:20,186 --> 00:01:22,757
The gate is not a model.

15
00:01:22,757 --> 00:01:24,043
Eight of eight.

16
00:01:24,043 --> 00:01:24,471
Pass.

17
00:01:24,471 --> 00:01:25,757
Try it yourself.

18
00:01:25,757 --> 00:01:32,614
Change one number in a verified
sentence, and the gate refuses it, right
in the browser.

19
00:01:32,614 --> 00:01:43,757
Live, not a replay: the same case in a
terminal, the model saying why it opens
each entry, ending on the record hash
the page shows.

20
00:01:43,757 --> 00:01:47,186
When the chart can't answer, it says so.

21
00:01:47,186 --> 00:01:54,043
Muse Wonder read all twenty-two entries
for the sepsis criterion, found nothing,
declined and named it.

22
00:01:54,043 --> 00:01:59,186
Here are nine runs using three Meta
models including the open-weights one.

23
00:01:59,186 --> 00:02:03,043
Agreement with the key, forty-five to
fifty of fifty.

24
00:02:03,043 --> 00:02:11,186
Not a single fabricated quote in a
sentence and using Spark 1.3 kept it
under a dollar a run.

25
00:02:11,186 --> 00:02:14,186
Three models, one case, side by side.

26
00:02:14,186 --> 00:02:18,900
And the writ, which is the draft that I
would sign.

27
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:25,757
Every sentence is footnoted to the entry
it quotes, with the plan's denial reason
fully answered.

28
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:30,043
This can be printed or saved, but
remains a draft.

29
00:02:30,043 --> 00:02:32,186
Nothing signed leaves the page.

30
00:02:32,186 --> 00:02:44,186
What I discovered: with a check outside
the model holding every sentence to the
record, these models draft a
consistently true and valid appeal in
seconds, for cents.

31
00:02:44,186 --> 00:02:51,471
I challenged the gate with nine and a
half thousand injected fabrications;
every hard one was caught.

32
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:56,186
Next, my own practice first, then a
method for prior authorizations.
